  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 134 PM MST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nSatellite imagery shows fast moving shortwave reaching the \nOregon coast this afternoon. Radar imagery already picking up \nprecipitation spreading across the central mountains and eastern\nhighlands. Snow levels remain high until frontal boundary \npushes through overnight, with QPF amounts orographically \nhelped. Heaviest precipitation is expected to fall during the \nevening and overnight for the central mountains, shifting to the\neastern highlands for the latter half of the night into early \nSaturday. Believe that there is too much orographic influence \nfrom the high-res RRFS runs into the QPF forecasts, with NBM \nmeans have increased slightly with the latest cycle. Overall \nsnow totals still look similar to previous forecasts, and \nslightly warmer temperatures and snow levels may be feeding into\nthat. Galena Summit still remains the pass of concern with \ntotal accumulation associated with the event right around 6\". \nThat said, the NBM does give roughly a 55-65% chance to exceed \nthat. And, with the slightly warmer temperatures and snow \nlevels, the expectation remains that snow amounts rapidly \ndecrease down to valley floors. Further east, the difference is \neven more dramatic. Forecast amounts remain 2-4\" over each of \nthe passes east of I-15, including Targee, Raynolds, Pine Creek \nand Emmigrant. Those heading over Teton Pass (RIW forecast area)\nshould be aware of potential travel impacts, however, with \nhigher amounts forecast at that elevation. Will continue to hold\non snow-related headlines. The other impact is the wind \nassociated with the frontal boundary as it dives southeast \nthrough the region overnight. Wind values have also increased \nslightly with this morning's model runs, with NBM now painting \nsome potential for gusts to exceed 50 mph. Thus have added the \nShoshone Lava Beds and the Lower Snake River Plain zones to the \nWIND ADVISORY. Northwest winds are less favorable for stronger \nwinds, so the advisory drops off by mid morning as the flow \nchanges. \n\nThe remainder of the forecast remains largely unchanged. A break\nis expected late in the day Saturday through Sunday, then the\nnext feature works into East Idaho beginning late Sunday. Liquid\nQPF amounts with the next system are lighter than tonight's\nfeature, but also favor better chances for the Snake River\nPlain. Snow levels range from 6000-6500 ft elevation, and most\nof the accumulations look to fall across the central mountains,\nabout 2-5\". The next system for Tuesday into Wednesday is still\nlooking like a split flow pattern with the main low digging well\nsouth of Idaho. If the split remains, most of the precipitation\nassociated should remain focused to the southern portions of\nEast Idaho. Progressive pattern then continues with passage of\nanother quick system late in the week.",
